# 100blackmagic_riscv_progbuf_dpc_and_busy.patch
#
# WHY THIS PATCH EXISTS
# ---------------------
# riscv_fault.md - the audit of this stack against RISC-V External Debug Support v0.13.2 -
# reports two HIGH findings in the RISC-V program buffer (abstractauto) paths:
#
#   F1  the streaming pipeline writes data0 / progbuf / abstractauto while an autoexec'd
#       abstract command can still be executing, and restores the bounce registers (a0/a1)
#       with no busy check, no verification and no diagnostic. Of command, abstractauto,
#       progbuf and the data registers the spec says "Accessing these registers while an
#       abstract command is executing causes cmderr to be set to 1 (busy) if it is 0" and
#       "Attempts to write them while busy is set does not change their value" (3.12.7,
#       3.12.8, 3.12.11, 3.12.12; the vendor documents the same rule at sdi.txt:1258). A write
#       the DM discards is silent: a lost a0 restore leaves a0 holding the advanced address
#       (one addi a0,a0,4 per executed program) and a lost abstractauto=0 leaves the stream
#       armed, so the *next* data0 access re-runs a stale command - during the register
#       restores. The ~37us SDI frame normally hides this (a progbuf run needs ~1us), which is
#       why it has not shown up in testing.
#
#   F2  dpc is never saved/restored around program buffer execution, which spec 3.7 requires:
#       "Executing the Program Buffer may clobber dpc. [...] The debugger must attempt to save
#       dpc between halting and executing a Program Buffer, and then restore dpc before
#       leaving Debug Mode." All four executors (riscv_csr_progbuf_read/_write,
#       riscv32_progbuf_mem_read/_write) saved only a0 (and a1), so on a part whose DM runs
#       the program buffer through the PC, *every* CSR access and every memory access would
#       move the PC. The only path that protected it was riscv32_run_stub() (patch 95).
#
# The streaming code fixed here came in with 85bis (there is no patch 86 - riscv_fault.md
# cites that number for 85bis), so this is a new patch rather than a rework of an old one.
#
# FIX
# ---
# F1 - riscv32_progbuf_quiesced_write() wraps every write the DM must not be busy for: it
#      calls riscv_command_wait_complete() (which waits for busy to clear *and* consumes
#      cmderr) and then writes. It is used for the progbuf neutralise, for dropping
#      abstractauto - which now happens even when the data phase gave up, so an armed stream
#      can no longer be left behind - and for both restores.
#      riscv32_progbuf_restore_gpr() puts a0/a1 back and checks the write landed by reading
#      the register back, retrying a bounded number of times before reporting on the probe's
#      log (the DMI link is known to drop the odd write: see the ch006 investigation in
#      ch32v0x_silicon_tests). The a0/a1 save buffers became 4 words wide so that a Hart
#      probed at 64-bit (the V003 is, see F7 of the audit) no longer has a 64-bit transfer
#      written through a 4-byte object. The failure is *not* turned into a GDB-visible error
#      here - that is F8, deliberately parked in patch 99 - what changes is that the
#      corruption can no longer happen silently.
#
# F2 - riscv_dpc_guard_begin()/_end() (riscv_debug.c) save dpc with an abstract command that
#      carries dpc itself and runs no program buffer of its own (the mechanism 3.7 asks for),
#      tried at the probed width and then at half that, and put it back after the run. They are
#      called by all four executors, with accesses to dpc itself exempt (protecting dpc by
#      rewriting dpc would undo a 'set $pc', and asking for dpc in order to read dpc would be
#      a loop).
#      The round trip is only paid on a Hart that needs it: the first execution compares dpc
#      before and after and records the answer (RV_DPC_GUARD_STABLE / _CLOBBERS, or _OPAQUE if
#      dpc cannot be read that way at all, which is reported once on the log). A part that
#      leaves dpc alone - the V003 does: reading it through the program buffer is how
#      riscv_pc_read() sees the right value - pays one read and one read-back, once, and
#      nothing from then on. A run that did not complete cleanly (bus error or exception inside
#      the program buffer) goes back to measuring, since that can move dpc where a clean run
#      does not.
#
# EVIDENCE
# --------
# Not yet measured on hardware: build only (gd32f303_48_inv_ws2812, the LN probe that talks to
# the CH32V003 over SDI, builds clean and with no new warnings) plus code review. To see it,
# watch the probe log (mon redirect 1) for the one-line measurement, and compare
# mon riscv_benchmark / memory reads before and after.
#
